# The window's chrome carries the app's primary actions. One catalog,
# one `primary` bit per action, and every host promotes the same first
# two in catalog preorder (docs/chrome-plan.md C2): the desktop's
# toolbar, the phones' top bar. Nothing here is a styling knob — the
# modern geometry is what having the bar produces.
expect label#0 "ready"
# BARE, like expect_menu_presentation: capacity k is the platform's own
# number, never kaya's, and this scene is compared byte-for-byte on
# every lane — so the step asserts the invariant (the promoted set is
# really in the chrome, the remainder is reachable) and never a count.
expect_toolbar
# The SEMANTIC ICON on the real chrome button, the same vocabulary the
# menu rows draw from (docs/styling-plan.md D6). Read off the button
# the platform rendered, never off the promotion list — the read that
# agrees with the model is the one that hid the iOS symbol gap for a
# whole milestone (docs/chrome/toolbar-repo.md §2.4).
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "done"
expect_toolbar_item "Find" "search"
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "enabled"

# ONE SOURCE OF TRUTH, ASSERTED BOTH WAYS. The app disables the MENU
# item through its bound signal and says nothing about any button; the
# toolbar button must follow, because it is the same item. The pair is
# the assertion: the chrome read and the menu read come from two
# different trees, so a lowering that kept its own enablement copy
# passes one and fails the other.
click button#0
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "disabled"
expect_menu "File>Save" disabled
click button#0
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "enabled"
expect_menu "File>Save" enabled
